  "name": "Question 22",
  "problem": "A particle of mass $32\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{\u2033}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\frac{t}{4}+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\frac{t}{4}$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\u03d5$ is the phase.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{\u2033}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{8}{32}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\u27f9\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\omega t+{\u03d5}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\omega t+{\u03d5}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\frac{t}{2}+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
